  "response": "Spatial pattern is the first thing a field diagnostician reads in a declining crop. Pathogen-caused decline typically appears in discrete patches that expand along planting rows over successive weeks, whereas nutrient deficiency symptoms appear uniformly across soil zones of similar texture and topography. Nutrient deficiencies spread from plant to plant through contact with soil water, a mode of transmission that cannot be reconciled with an even expression of symptoms wherever soil texture and topography are alike.\n\nDiagnosticians split the taproot and lower stem of affected plants to examine the vascular tissue for discoloration. Foliar morphology is read alongside such internal inspection: potassium deficiency in soybean produces chlorosis and necrosis along the margins of upper leaves, and potassium deficiency produces dark necrotic fungal lesions on the soybean taproot, a root pathology that cannot be squared with a mineral disorder whose expression is confined to marginal scorch of the leaf. Laboratory support is assembled in parallel with the field examination. The soil test reported exchangeable potassium levels for the affected area of the field. Sectioning of the taproots and lower stems showed that Fusarium virguliforme was present in the root tissue of the declining plants, and the reported exchangeable values indicated that soil potassium in the affected area was below the critical level for soybean. At least one of those two conditions obtained in that part of the field, fungal colonization of the roots or a sub-critical potassium supply, and perhaps both obtained together.\n\nCulture work on the symptomatic tissue yields a result that admits no middle ground. Either no plant pathogen was isolated from the symptomatic root tissue, or at least one plant pathogen was isolated from it; one of the two outcomes must be the case, and the two cannot hold together. Which outcome the plates deliver determines whether the investigation proceeds to inoculation work or turns instead to the fertility program and the soil chemistry of the site.\n\nWhen growers and agronomists advance opposing accounts, the matter is settled by moving from correlation to demonstrated causation under controlled conditions. That Fusarium virguliforme is the causal agent of sudden death syndrome in soybean is confirmed by greenhouse inoculation trials, in which the foliar symptoms observed in the field were reproduced. On the strength of that reproduction of field symptoms under controlled inoculation, the extension diagnostic review board concluded that the decline was caused by Fusarium virguliforme rather than by potassium deficiency. A ruling of this kind carries weight because it rests on an agent that was both recovered and shown to reproduce the disorder in healthy plants.",
